 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Compact Node Index Texture Generation
-This variant addresses the loss of original pixel position information during GPU-to-CPU texture transfer and reduction processes [S2, unofficial]. It encodes ray pixel positions and node indices into a compacted texture to allow the CPU to recover actual node indices [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Mechanism:** During texture transfer, 20 bits of a 32-bit entry are used to encode the pixel position of the ray, alongside 12 node entries per list [S2, unofficial]. After reduction, each pixel contains a ray pixel-position and a bitmask [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Layout:** The final compacted texture is a single RGBA32 texture, storing indices of four nodes per pixel [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Parameters:** The bitmask usually contains 2-3 non-zero entries [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Pitfall:** If the bitmask exceeds the limit (more than 4 nodes), requests are automatically postponed to the next frame [S2, unofficial].
+A variant of the storage layout used during GPU-to-CPU data transfer. It addresses the loss of original pixel position during texture reduction. It encodes the pixel position (using 20 bits of a 32-bit entry) alongside node indices (12 bits). The final texture is a single RGBA32 texture storing indices of four nodes per pixel. The bitmask usually contains 2-3 non-zero entries. If the limit of 4 nodes is exceeded, requests are postponed to the next frame [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory:** Requires two 2D textures for the knots (scalar and distance) plus one 2D index texture [S1, unofficial]. The size of the knots textures depends on the total number of intersections across all rays [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Enables fast culling of non-intersecting rays via the index texture's $b$ channel [S1, unofficial]. The packed layout improves texture cache coherence during ray traversal [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** The method assumes a pre-determined traversal order [S1, unofficial]. The compact variant [S2, unofficial] has a hard limit on nodes per pixel (4) and may require multi-frame processing for complex intersections [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory**: Requires two 2D textures (Index and Knots). The Index Texture size is fixed by the PRI resolution. The Knots Texture size depends on the number of intersections (knots) per ray.
+*   **Performance**: Allows for efficient texture lookups on the GPU. The packed layout ensures coherent memory access patterns during traversal [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Precision**: High precision is maintained by using separate float textures for scalar and distance values [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations**: The layout is static once generated. Dynamic updates require rebuilding the textures. The compact node index variant has a hard limit of 4 nodes per pixel; exceeding this causes frame postponement [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The PRI Texture Storage method was introduced in the context of GPU-based unstructured grid volume rendering to overcome bottlenecks in mapping irregular data to hardware [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the Pixel Ray Image (PRI) concept [S1, unofficial]. The compact node index variant was developed in the context of ray-guided streaming for voxel rendering, aiming to efficiently transfer reduced data from GPU to CPU [S2, unofficial].
+The PRI Texture Storage method was introduced in the context of GPU-based unstructured grid volume rendering. It was presented in the paper "An Image-Based Modelling Approach To GPU-based Unstructured Grid Volume Rendering" [S1, S2, unofficial]. The compact node index variant was later described in "Ray-Guided Streaming for Efficient and Detailed Voxel Rendering" [S3, unofficial].
 
